#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167762} :activity/id #uuid "3c9e36db-811c-5e22-ab47-cb6642c31eaf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050757 :topic/id #uuid "643ac99b-01f2-533f-b1bc-9babbc0cef82" :topic/math-academy-id 3284} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168075 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials, each resulting in success or failure, and with a probability of success $p.$ Let the random variable $X$ represent the number of Bernoulli trials *until the first success*.\n\nIt can be shown that $X$ follows a **geometric distribution** and has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/13/13d0eefea25458f7778607118a7673506900d72ee5b8a41826378c8737317e9e.svg)\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- The factor $(1-p)^{x-1}$ represents the probability of getting exactly $(x-1)$ failures, each with probability $(1-p).$\n\n- The factor ${\\color{purple}{p}}$ represents the probability of getting a single success.\n\n- The condition $x=1,2,3,\\ldots$ indicates that the sequence of Bernoulli trials terminates after a minimum of $1$ trial, and there is no maximum number of trials.\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a geometric distribution, we say it is a geometric random variable and  write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fdc5cdcc-48e5-5c68-a8b0-d4a179a4f320" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5055 :tutorial/title "The Probability Mass Function of a Geometric Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "1d4c3df6-592f-5405-bb8e-5da92b770183" :step/math-academy-id 31642 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166665 :question/id #uuid "e3df676d-e1cc-584e-8b14-a6191a8d6831" :question/math-academy-id "e-8553"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d83ab99-0e27-5989-9a57-6b4a192fa4a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490323}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "bea180fa-6a68-5aff-b1e1-a8d6a9c237fe" :step/math-academy-id 31788 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167668}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166666 :question/id #uuid "f795e566-5cdf-5970-bf94-5b1c838b69f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8554"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120381af-c472-5894-8b19-da2ab61d3a7e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532716}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "eb83b5c2-40bc-5557-850a-406d7b909d09" :step/math-academy-id 31789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166667 :question/id #uuid "c23e8b4a-dad2-5c6a-95ee-2a439343ded0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8555"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c566525f-9b0f-586f-ba60-277400cb25dd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532711}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Not Zero"} :step/id #uuid "ec4d9548-3439-5b0b-a9f4-7e116d92445d" :step/math-academy-id 31790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166668 :question/id #uuid "c798d6ea-a258-5a73-8cd2-366e1b3e9db0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8556"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "215130d1-96ea-5f88-8de0-d73758ea8344" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532675}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "563feb69-65ee-57de-a416-0f33d12b88c6" :step/math-academy-id 31791 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168108 :tutorial/content "You might be wondering where the geometric distribution gets its name. The answer is that the probability mass function forms a geometric sequence! \n\nSuppose that $X\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).$ Then, the probability mass function for this geometric distribution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-p)^{x-1}p \\\\ f(x) & =p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\ {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{x}} & ={{p}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}\\cdot ({{1-p}_{⏟}}_{r})^{x-1} \\\\ {a}_{x} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we see that the probability mass function can be interpreted as a geometric sequence where the first term is $a_1=p,$ and the common ratio is $r=1-p.$\n\nMoreover, notice that $f(x)$ satisfies all the criteria to be a probability mass function, namely\n\n1\\. $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n2\\. $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nCriterion 1 is clearly true because $0 \\leq p \\leq 1.$ \n\nTo show that criterion 2 is true, we simply need to calculate the sum of this infinite geometric series, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x) & ={\\sum }_{x=1}^{\\infty }p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{1-(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{p} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is indeed a probability distribution." :tutorial/id #uuid "0ac9b8c4-e381-5d24-b7f2-6e0cf660a14a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5365 :tutorial/title "Showing That a Geometric Distribution Is a Probability Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "4eb95a11-d9d7-553d-a1ee-58106336ccf8" :step/math-academy-id 33495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168131 :tutorial/content "A **Bernoulli trial** is a random experiment whose outcome can be interpreted as either \"success\" or \"failure.\" \n\nFor example, suppose we throw a die and define a \"success\" as the event of getting a six and a \"failure\" as getting any other number. Then, the throw of a die can be thought of as a Bernoulli trial with the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{six}) = \\dfrac16, \\qquad P(\\textrm{not six}) = \\dfrac56\n$$\n\nNow suppose that a fair die is repeatedly thrown *until* we get a six. What is the probability that we need to throw the die precisely ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ times to get our first six?\n\nFirst, notice that each throw of the die is a Bernoulli trial. And since we throw the die repeatedly until we get a six, we have a *sequence* of Bernoulli trials. \n\nLet $X$ be the number of times the die is thrown until we get a six. We wish to compute $P(X = {\\color{blue}{3}}).$ \n\nTo get our first six on the *third* throw, we need to throw \"not a six\" on the *first two* throws, followed by a \"six\" on the *final* throw. Assuming that each throw of the die is independent, by the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =P(\\text{not six})\\cdot P(\\text{not six})\\cdot P(\\text{six}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{216}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe random variable $X$ in this example follows a so-called **geometric distribution**. Let's now discuss geometric distributions a little more generally." :tutorial/id #uuid "3201acc8-7bbc-59e6-9a50-f9c5f19899e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6168 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8ed0465c-f38c-56a2-acbf-cf8b05298867" :step/math-academy-id 37401 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168132 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ be the number of times a fair die is thrown until we get a six. Since we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials, each with a probability of success $p=\\dfrac16,$ the number of throws until the first six can be modeled as a geometric random variable, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac16\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting our first six on the $\\color{blue}3$rd roll is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{3-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{216}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with our previous result." :tutorial/id #uuid "95415f67-f3f8-5c48-9178-812ddf3830e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6169 :tutorial/title "Applying the Probability Mass Function of a Geometric Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "0fec3647-5057-5207-8619-9dd7001d1b85" :step/math-academy-id 37402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167667}}] :activity/title "The Geometric Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167045}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532741} :activity/id #uuid "eccbce88-476f-56f7-afed-2a82763ecdf2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050757 :topic/id #uuid "643ac99b-01f2-533f-b1bc-9babbc0cef82" :topic/math-academy-id 3284} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c94ff5-e094-5fec-a663-783e3db82388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9493" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532225}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532225} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc50829-ddbd-58f0-8a60-4f7a72a20ec5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a330685-db48-5728-bb5b-a1b5a0526e38" :question/math-academy-id "q-332929" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.63),$ compute $P(X<4).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X<4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.63),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.37)}^{x-1}(0.63), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(0.37)}^{0}(0.63)+{(0.37)}^{1}(0.63)+{(0.37)}^{2}(0.63) \\\\  & ≈0.9493.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0cab9f07-f53a-5d32-a373-e6bac7fd4446" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be3563b-85a4-5927-8f14-75809dbb4706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3263" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532090} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9c899d-e556-5912-93c1-9e3ca0347975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3074" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532104} {:answer/id #uuid "551c66e8-4471-5f2d-9a69-c55890cd1f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3182" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532116} {:answer/id #uuid "68de0d90-b739-525e-a634-18b6e92914fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2901" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532167} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1a9056-6d3f-565a-ae29-98e576722262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532377}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532104} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd41d07a-8118-5a4f-af31-10c9c686bf80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7dd6d27-99b8-51e0-8f07-28863587b350" :question/math-academy-id "q-97161" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.1),$ compute $P(4<X\\le 10)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.1),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.9)}^{x-1}(0.1), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4<X\\le 10) & =P(X\\in \\{5,6,7,8,9,10\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=5)+P(X=6)+P(X=7)+ \\\\  & \\,P(X=8)+P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6)+f(7)+f(8)+f(9)+f(10) \\\\  & ={(0.9)}^{4}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{5}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{6}(0.1)+ \\\\  & \\,{(0.9)}^{7}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{8}(0.1)+{(0.9)}^{9}(0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.3074.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "646fc0d5-2e80-5a45-9947-e31e4a48e0e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce64594-4d44-5fbb-916a-9540f6c9aad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1452" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532092} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc4bd4f-eaf1-5902-990b-f37f8dc13633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2186" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532211} {:answer/id #uuid "d55089b5-7956-5fa0-8147-9220f21301db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1630" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532391} {:answer/id #uuid "e07dadf3-01af-5381-bcd2-159701c2246f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1965" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532413} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c17dfa-72d1-519b-a04e-f7cb863e8d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1406" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532415}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532415} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df74f69-e9ee-5e86-8090-3b562192d1f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "88f1dddc-e661-5d20-9bf7-86d209c9d4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-97183" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{5}{8}),$ compute $P(X\\ge 3)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{5}{8}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x-1}(\\frac{5}{8}), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 3.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{8})}^{0}(\\frac{5}{8})+{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{1}(\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\  & ≈0.8594.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8594 \\\\  & =0.1406.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b8587c43-02d1-5bdb-a188-5183461899c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532751}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e79b2f4c-88fd-5171-9805-d4c810b84f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532434} :answer-field/id #uuid "635112d3-93b9-5181-b8a6-34ea068cbead" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ef957d04-4e42-5090-b9a6-8e161a911753" :question/math-academy-id "q-332586" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{4}{5}),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(X\\le 3).$\n\n$P(X\\le 3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{4}{5}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{4}{5}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{0}(\\frac{4}{5})+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{1}(\\frac{4}{5})+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c3439878-ff61-50b6-9b9a-bbd0ad959b16" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84d4b28b-adba-57ec-9abd-1c6eab03e465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532232} :answer-field/id #uuid "21503cab-a324-5fe0-bffe-94e6d9d3497e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "82d7f69c-d6ad-553d-a4a8-4fc134f8a8d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335528" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7),$ compute the value of $P(X>5)$ round to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X>5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>5.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X>5)=1-P(X\\le 5)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{0}(0.7)+(0.3)^{1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3}(0.7)+(0.3)^{4}(0.7) \\\\  & =0.99757.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>5) & =1-P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =1-0.99757 \\\\  & ≈0.0024.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ceafd4e3-8a65-51ca-9ad8-82f0439b1d22"}] :activity/title "The Geometric Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531936}]}
